{%- comment -%}
  The one close control, in the shop's chosen form — Theme settings → Buttons →
  Close controls. Text is the default: a "Close" pill, the design's own idiom.
  Icon swaps every instance for the same bordered disc holding the theme's
  close glyph; the accessible name stays "Close" either way.

  Callers pass *placement*, not appearance: a context class that positions the
  control (`.drawer__close` in a drawer head, `.quick-view__close` in the
  panel corner) and the attributes that wire it (`data-overlay-close`,
  autofocus, tips). Appearance belongs to the mode classes here —
  `.overlay-head__close` for the pill, `.overlay-close--icon` for the disc —
  so a context class must never carry visual style of its own again; that is
  how quick view ended up an icon while the drawer was a word.

  Deliberately not `snippets/button.liquid`: a close is a purpose-built
  interface control, the R16 exemption, though its corner radius follows the
  global button shape so Rounded means rounded here too.

  Accepts:
    class       - context placement classes.
    attributes  - captured attribute string (data-overlay-close etc.).
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if settings.close_style == 'icon' -%}
  <button class="overlay-close--icon {{ class }}" type="button" {{ attributes }}>
    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'navigation.close' | t }}</span>
    {% render 'icon', icon: 'close' %}
  </button>
{%- else -%}
  <button class="overlay-head__close {{ class }}" type="button" {{ attributes }}>
    {{- 'navigation.close' | t -}}
  </button>
{%- endif -%}
